# The pinned BLIS stack: the ONE source of truth for "which simulator produced these reports".
#
# Read by scripts/build_catalog.py (the `inference-sim` version pill in the site footer) and by
# .github/workflows/generate-report.yml (which clones exactly these refs before running a search).
# SETUP.md documents the same pins for a human setting the skill up by hand.
#
# Shell-sourceable (`set -a; . pins.env; set +a`) and parsed as plain KEY=VALUE lines by Python,
# so keep it to unquoted, comment-or-assignment lines.
#
# Bumping a pin: change it here, then re-run `python3 -I scripts/test_build.py` (it checks this
# file against SETUP.md and the workflow) and rebuild the site.

# inference-sim release whose binary prices every run. BLIS_SHA is the commit the tag pointed at
# when it was pinned; the tag is NOT immutable upstream, so SETUP.md verifies the SHA on clone.
#
# BLIS_SHA is a COMMIT, and it is checked as one. There is deliberately no expected binary digest
# here: the same tag builds to different bytes on different toolchains and platforms (v0.12.0 is
# recorded with two different digests across the committed reports, and gave two more again when
# rebuilt locally), so a pinned digest would be wrong for every contributor but its author. The
# runners resolve the binary's SOURCE commit instead and compare that -- run_search.py's
# blis_pin_warning / _blis_source_commit, guarded by test_search.py and test_build.py. CI refuses
# a moved tag outright (both workflows verify on clone); a local sweep warns, because testing
# against an unreleased simulator is how a pin bump gets evaluated in the first place.
BLIS_REF=v0.15.0
BLIS_SHA=762f1605ff27cd825d6b2cb2d9e991cd4b864c91

# WHAT MOVING THE PIN v0.12.0 -> v0.14.0 CHANGED (issue #23). Verified by building and running
# both binaries, not by reading release notes:
#
#   * v0.14.0 moves to blis-schemas v0.8.0, where a pool's `engine:` is no longer a fixed struct
#     but an OPAQUE block (deployment.EngineBlock) opened by the engine model that the scenario's
#     engine_version selects -- now blis-vllm v0.32.0's rules/engineconfig.Engine. That is an
#     architectural change, not a version bump: the engine surface moved repositories.
#   * So all 21 names that validate_experiment.ENGINE_NOT_WIRED / run_blis.UNSUPPORTED used to
#     refuse now DECODE. Measured: v0.12.0 fails each at deployment decode ("field watermark not
#     found in type deployment.Engine"); v0.14.0 accepts all 21.
#   * The bump therefore turned a LOUD failure into a SILENT one, which is the thing to know
#     about it. Only 3 of the 20 newly-decodable knobs change a number: max_num_active_seqs,
#     max_num_scheduled_tokens and frontend. The other 11 run to completion and measure
#     byte-identical -- the kernel cost model has no term for them. Measured per knob with two
#     contrasting values on deepseek-v4-pro/h200 (tp2 dp4 chatbot, c=8 and c=256),
#     qwen3-14b/h100 (tp4) and deepseek-v4.1-flash/h200 (tp2 dp4), beside a max_num_seqs control
#     that moved every time. They stay refused, now as ENGINE_INERT: a sweep that completes and
#     reports equal numbers costs the whole campaign AND reads as a finding.
#     The measured split lives in validate_experiment.py: ENGINE_INERT (11), ENGINE_NOT_MODELLED
#     (3), ENGINE_CONDITIONAL (4) -- 18 refusals, where v0.12.0 had 21.
#   * max_num_active_seqs is the one real lever the bump adds, and it only bites once there is a
#     queue: inert at c=8, and 2405.3 -> 808.4 tok/s at c=256 with 8 active seqs (v4-pro/h200).
#   * Enum checking did NOT move with the fields: mla_prefill_backend: flashattn and
#     linear_backend: cutlass are invented values that both ran rc=0 under v0.14.0. Another
#     reason to keep them refused rather than merely documented.
#   * ENGINE_KNOBS needed no change: its 51 names match engineconfig.Engine's 51 top-level yaml
#     tags exactly at v0.32.0, in both directions.
#   * v0.32.0's rules make `max_model_len` MANDATORY ("must be stated; an unstated or auto window
#     is the model's or the memory's"). Every spec now states it. That also settles item 3 of #23:
#     the two AgentX specs' 262144 is still required, and is now required of every spec.
#   * run_blis.PD_MUST_MATCH was wrong in BOTH directions against compat/build/pools.go at
#     v0.32.0 and is corrected: requireSameKVCacheFormat compares cache_dtype plus the three
#     mamba_* fields, and does NOT compare quantization.
#
# WHAT MOVING ON AGAIN v0.14.0 -> v0.15.0 CHANGED. v0.15.0 is the Muse-Spark release ("Run
# Muse-Spark-Open-816B-A42B at v0.32.0: pipelined engines, every layout on three clusters"), and
# supporting that model is why this pin moved. Verified by building v0.15.0 and running it:
#
#   * PIPELINE PARALLELISM IS NOW PRICED, and that is the headline for this repo. run_blis refused
#     pp>1 outright ("the pinned kernel does not price pipeline parallelism") in two places. Under
#     kernel v0.5.0 it is priced and both refusals are obsolete, so they are gone. Measured on the
#     Muse armB layout (h100 roce, 2 nodes, 16 GPUs, EP on, 8 requests of 4096/64):
#     tp8 x pp2 = 96.80 tok/s against tp16 x pp1 = 99.47 at the SAME footprint -- the two layouts
#     price apart, so pp is a real lever and not merely accepted. The gap's size belongs to that
#     workload and is not a bound; sweep pp against the topology.
#   * cost-model-fabric is the coefficient set REGISTRY_REF v0.3.0 adds, and run_blis's
#     COEFFICIENTS now names it (all 40 upstream Muse scenarios do). It is NOT enforced: a
#     scenario omitting it runs rc=0 and simply prices the interconnect differently (48.61 vs
#     48.38 tok/s on armB, workload held fixed), so leaving it out is a silent mispricing -- the
#     same hazard shape as the v0.14.0 inert knobs.
#   * blis-schemas v0.8.0 -> v0.10.0 and blis-vllm v0.32.0 -> v0.32.0-blis.1. Note the SUFFIX:
#     the engine model is a blis fork tag now, not the plain upstream v0.32.0.
#   * stdout is a Result document (upstream's `feat(output)`): run/replay emit one JSON (or YAML,
#     --format) document instead of the flat MetricsOutput dict. This DID need a parser change,
#     and it is the sharpest hazard in the bump. _find_metrics_dicts still found a dict carrying
#     tokens_per_sec (engine.cluster.whole_run), so metrics never read as None and nothing failed
#     loudly -- but the aggregate was RESTRUCTURED around it:
#       - itl_* was renamed step_interval_* (result_inputs.go fills whole_run's step_interval_*
#         from the fields MetricsOutput's itl_* held), so itl_p99_ms was absent;
#       - injected/completed_requests, still_*, timed_out_requests moved to the document's own
#         `requests` block under shorter names, so _completion_issue could never fire and an
#         overloaded run ranked as a clean capacity point;
#       - per-instance rows did NOT move: engine.instances.<id> is still a verbatim MetricsOutput.
#     That last asymmetry is what makes it dangerous. A MULTI-instance run still resolved ITL off
#     its per-instance rows, while a SINGLE-instance run (no instances block) had no itl_* at all,
#     so every ITL SLO failed as "declared but not measured" and the sweep reported 0 of 24
#     servable -- a total loss of feasibility judgment that reads as a finding, not an error. The
#     same sweep reports 17 of 24 servable once the fields are remapped. run_blis._flatten_result
#     does that remapping, guarded by test_run_blis.ResultDocument.
#     Note which window: whole_run is the WHOLE RUN, which is what every earlier pin printed and
#     what the committed ledgers hold. Top-level `metrics` is the MEASURED WINDOW and its itl_* is
#     FormulaTPOTExclFirst (per-request TPOT), a different population -- 294.03 vs 33.87 ms on one
#     8-request Muse run. Sourcing itl_* from there would silently re-baseline every latency in
#     the repo against 12 committed reports.
#   * The run CLI changed shape: --scenario is a FILE NAME within --scenarios (a directory), and
#     --registry replaces --coefficients. run_blis already invokes it this way.
#   * CATALOG_REF must be 0.5.0: the three Muse entries (muse-spark-open-816b-a42b, its -nvfp4
#     twin and muse-spark-open-dspark) do not exist in 0.4.0 at all.
#   * REGISTRY_REF must be v0.3.0: every Muse scenario names cost-model-fabric, and v0.2.0 has no
#     such coefficient set. This is a hard requirement, not a preference.
#   * Upstream refuses one combination, so do not chase it: H200 DEP8 + DSpark at max_model_len
#     262144 is rejected with vLLM's own message. Their 40-scenario matrix documents it.
#
# KERNEL_REF moves with it: v0.15.0's go.mod pins blis-latency-kernel v0.5.0.

# blis-latency-kernel, pinned in inference-sim's own go.mod at BLIS_REF (recorded, not cloned).
KERNEL_REF=v0.5.0

# The catalog + registry the pinned binary runs against.
#
# CATALOG_REF was 0.3.0 while the checkout these reports ran against was 0.4.0 -- the `resolved`
# block in every scenario.json recorded the real commit (ba542f0, describe 0.4.0) and that is how
# the mismatch was caught (namasl review). 0.4.0 adds workloads/sharegpt.yaml and changes nothing
# else, so no AgentX number moves; the pin is corrected to the version that actually ran rather
# than re-running against 0.3.0 to match a label.
#
# The registry MUST be v0.2.0 or later:
# it carries the cost-model-memory coefficient set the kernel backend needs AND the acceptance/
# profiles that price speculative decoding (v0.1.1 has cost-model-memory but no acceptance/).
CATALOG_REF=0.5.0
REGISTRY_REF=v0.3.0
